  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder air will be accompanied by scattered wet snow \nand rain showers through this weekend with minor snow accumulations\nexpected across the higher terrain.\n\nThe low-level flow will slowly back tonight as a surface low \nmoves off the east coast. An area of frontogenesis aloft exists\nnorth of the surface low, with associated focused areas of\nprecip primarily east of our CWA as of 12:30 AM EST Friday. A \nshortwave trough will move across the region tonight, with \nmoisture pulling away from the region. The atmospheric column\nwill continue to cool via CAA at the surface and aloft, the\nwet-bulb effect, and nocturnal so that any lingering rain across\nthe Western Southern Tier, Finger Lakes, and North-Central NY \nwill change to wet snow overnight. A coating of snow\naccumulation is possible across the higher terrain, especially\nthat of the Finger Lakes Region. Upslope flow-induced and lake-induced\nsnow showers will develop late tonight across the Chautauqua \nRidge and a coating of snow accumulation is possible by daybreak\nFriday.\n\nThe set-up will become favorable for upslope flow-induced and \nlake-effect precipitation Friday through Friday night generally\nsouth of the eastern Great Lakes. However, a lack of synoptic \nmoisture should prevent the formation of any organized \nprecipitation. Cold air advection and low atmospheric melting \nlevels will support rain mixing with or changing to wet snow at \ntimes at lower elevations and primarily wet snow at higher \nelevations. Very little or no snow accumulation is expected at \nlower elevations, but additional snow accumulations of a coating\nto 2\" are expected along the Chautauqua Ridge and Bristol \nHills.\n\nA sharp mid/upper-level trough axis is expected to approach and\nthen swing south-southeastward through our region Saturday \nthrough Saturday night. This will support an uptick in \nprecipitation across Western and Central NY, however this uptick\nshould be fairly short-lived. A quick coating of snow \naccumulation is possible at lower elevations, while an \nadditional coating to 1\" of snow accumulation is expected at \nhigher elevations of Western NY, the Finger Lakes Region, and \nNorth-Central NY, including the Tug Hill Plateau.\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and aloft builds from the west on\nSunday through Sunday night, while subtle shortwave trough axes\nembedded in the northerly mid/upper-level flow traverse Western\nand Central NY. Much drier air behind Saturday night's \nmid/upper-level trough axis passage will expand through the \ndendritic snow growth zone Sunday morning, with any lingering\nupslope flow-induced and lake-induced wet snow and/or rain\nshowers amidst north-northwesterly mean low-level flow ending \nthrough the morning hours. A few wet snow and/or rain showers \nmay linger across Central NY into the early afternoon hours. The\nstill cool northwesterly to north-northwesterly mean low-level \nflow and shallow low-level moisture on the synoptic-scale will \nmaintain a wealth of upslope flow-induced and lake-effect cloud\ncover through the afternoon and overnight time period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with \ntemperatures trending warmer by midweek.\n\nAn expansive ridge at the surface and aloft will continue to \nbuild from the Great Plains and across the Great Lakes and Ohio\nValley early next week. This feature will bring a return to dry\nconditions, with perhaps a few partly to mostly sunny days. \nTemperatures will remain cool ahead of the surface high, but as \nthis surface high slides eastward, and a stronger southerly flow\nensues, afternoon temperatures will rise back into the lower \n50s by Tuesday or Wednesday of next week...with the warming \ntrend to well above normal temperatures continuing into \nThursday.",
